## Natural vs Lab — The 1ct Cost Gap

A 1ct GIA natural round starts at $3,230 in certified inventory. A 1ct IGI lab-grown round of equivalent grade (G-VS2) runs approximately $600–$900 — roughly 73–81% less.

That savings gap is real. It is also permanent. Lab prices have dropped 60%+ in the last 3 years and are not expected to recover. If you buy a 1ct lab-grown round today, its resale value in 5 years will be near zero. If you buy a 1ct GIA natural round today, it holds the commodity price of its certificate.

| Option | 1ct Round Stone | Cert | Approx Price | Resale in 5yr |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Natural | G-VS2 Excellent | GIA | [$3,230](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29090690?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | Partial value retained |
| Lab-grown | G-VS2 Excellent | IGI | ~$700–$900 | Near zero |
| Natural | G-VS1 Excellent | GIA | [$3,300](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29161966?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | Partial value retained |
| Natural | F-VS2 Excellent | GIA | [$3,490](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28215109?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | Strong resale |

**What this means:** If you plan to keep the ring for life, natural GIA is the better stone. If this is a wear-it-daily purchase with no long-term resale intent, lab at $700–$900 paired with a $1,000 setting = a $1,700–$1,900 total ring. It is indistinguishable to the naked eye. That is a legitimate path. Verify the IGI lab certificate at [igi.org](https://www.igi.org/) before purchase.

**How to buy a lab-grown 1ct for a men's ring:** Search the same retailer for "1ct round, G color, VS2 clarity, Excellent cut, IGI cert." Filter by IGI only — not GIA, not AGS. GIA does not certify lab-grown stones in the same grading format. IGI is the standard for lab. Confirm the stone is "lab-grown" in the cert, not "natural." Then pair it with any solitaire setting on this list that accepts a 6.4mm round head. The jeweler will not treat it differently during setting — the stone looks and handles identically to natural.

> "The lab question comes up in almost every conversation now. I tell every client the same thing: the stone is not the investment — the experience is. If the $2,500 you save on a lab stone goes into a better setting, a better metal, or a vacation you take while wearing the ring, that is a good trade. If you are buying natural because you want something that holds value, then you need to hold it for 10+ years and buy GIA-certified Excellent cut. There is no middle path where you buy natural and expect short-term appreciation."
> — Farzana Hasan, diamond expert | [About Farzana](/about-farzana/)

**Verdict:** Natural for investment or sentimental permanence. Lab for maximum carat at minimum cost with no long-term asset expectation. See the full [men's lab-grown diamond ring guide](/mens-lab-grown-diamond-ring) for IGI grades and current pricing across setting types.

